  "difficulty": "Advanced",
  "category": "Safety-Critical Systems",
  "scenario": "A junior engineer modeled an Air Traffic Control system with 5 serious UML violations: (1) Tower Supervisor should specialize Air Traffic Controller, not the reverse; (2) Radar Sensor is an Actor and cannot have an «include» relationship with Track Aircraft; (3) Log Flight Telemetry is mandatory for Track Aircraft, not an optional «extend»; (4) Issue Storm Reroute optionally extends Track Aircraft, so its arrow direction is backwards; and (5) Authorize Emergency Landing must «include» Clear Runway, not the other way around.",
  "goal": "Find and fix all 5 broken relationships while preserving the valid Associations (Controller -> Track Aircraft, Tower Supervisor -> Authorize Emergency Landing, Weather Feed -> Issue Storm Reroute).",
  "learningObjective": "Audit complex Use Case diagrams for inverted generalizations, actor-to-use-case stereotype misuse, and reversed «include»/«extend» semantics.",
  "hints": [
    "Generalization: Tower Supervisor inherits from Air Traffic Controller (Supervisor -> Controller).",
    "Actors only use Associations: replace Radar Sensor «include» Track Aircraft with an Association.",
    "Track Aircraft mandatorily includes Log Flight Telemetry (Track Aircraft --«include»--> Log Flight Telemetry).",
    "Issue Storm Reroute optionally extends Track Aircraft (Issue Storm Reroute --«extend»--> Track Aircraft), and Authorize Emergency Landing --«include»--> Clear Runway."
  ],
  "solutionExplanation": "Tower Supervisor specializes Air Traffic Controller; Radar Sensor connects via Association; Track Aircraft «includes» Log Flight Telemetry; Issue Storm Reroute «extends» Track Aircraft; and Authorize Emergency Landing «includes» Clear Runway.",
